From: JiaY-shi <shi05275@163.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 13:34:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: honor firmware peer limits and own pending MLO peer IDs

The valid ML peer ID range is platform dependent and is reported by
firmware in the MLO setup complete event. The upstream driver ignores this
field and allocates IDs from the compile-time maximum, starting at zero.
Current Qualcomm firmware reserves ID zero and expects the host to stay
inside the advertised range. Sending an invalid ID can leave the cross-chip
peer mapping inconsistent.

Qualcomm firmware can reject a peer association when the ML peer ID is
still present in its peer table. The peer assoc confirmation includes a
status for this case, but ath12k currently ignores it and continues
configuring the peer as though association succeeded. TIP carries a stop-
gap fix which parses this status and fails the station transition.

An ML peer ID is safe to reuse only after every link peer has been deleted
from firmware. ath12k currently discards the result of the MLO peer delete
operation and unconditionally releases a host-allocated ID. It also loses
cleanup failures while rolling back peer creation. A late or missing peer
unmap can therefore allow a new station to reuse an ID which firmware still
owns, matching the duplicate-ID firmware failure described by Qualcomm's
peer assoc status fix.

Validate both link maps and the current mac80211 link address before
reporting low ACK. Cross-check a datapath peer, when present, against the
control-plane station, vdev and link. Keep legacy address checks and the
existing reason-specific event handling.

Merged from:
  322-ath12k-honor-firmware-ml-peer-id-limit.patch
  323-wifi-ath12k-handle-peer-assoc-status.patch
  324-wifi-ath12k-quarantine-unconfirmed-ml-peer-ids.patch
  325-wifi-ath12k-validate-mlo-peer-kickout-events.patch

